+### Internal prefill checkpoints
+
+Newer vLLM versions can write an internal checkpoint into a slot whose physical
+column denotes a later boundary. For example, a slot at column 3 with block size
+640 can hold the state at token 1920, rather than token 2560. The connector must
+not infer the key from that column.
+
+For align-mode Mamba groups with internal checkpoints, ordinary stores null the
+state group and retain the attention data. The scheduler's
+`kv_connector_block_state.boundary_state_offloads` supplies the exact
+`(group_id, block_id, boundary_tokens)` handoffs. Each chunk-aligned boundary is
+stored as a separate state-only operation; unrelated groups are null. These
+stores may fill earlier chunks even after the ordinary store cursor advanced.
+This reuses the existing separate-object-group protocol. It requires a server
+chunk equal to the Mamba block size; finer boundaries are skipped, never rounded.
+
+The connector pins the source blocks before returning scheduler metadata.
+Workers order their reads after model/CoW writes and retain the IPC event until
+the transfer future completes. Completion counts accumulate across workers and
+steps; the last worker releases the pin. Request completion and preemption do
+not release that ownership. An unhealthy heartbeat is not completion of an
+already submitted copy. Pending jobs keep the engine polling even when no model
+requests remain. State checkpoints are stored eagerly even if attention offload
+uses a lazy policy. Attention-only and state-only operations do not advertise
+model-wide cache-store events; the normal lookup still checks all required groups.
+
+KV cache events must be disabled for exact-checkpoint groups: the MP server's
+event log does not join attention and recurrent object-group completions into a
+complete model prefix. This restriction does not disable events for other models.
+An RPC exception does not prove device work stopped. Polling then raises and keeps
+every outstanding job and event pinned, rather than acknowledging unsafe reuse.
+A terminal `False` transfer result is safe to acknowledge. Forced worker/server
+failure recovery still needs device-level validation; this path does not infer a
+safe DMA fence from a missed heartbeat or a request cancellation.
+
+When upgrading a deployment that previously stored internal checkpoints by
+physical position, start with an empty external cache. Existing objects may
+already contain a state under the wrong boundary key.
+
